<article>
<h1>Block Connections: Backend</h1>
<p class="why">Why this matters: every bug involving invisible wires, ghost connections, or broken save/load traces back to these five classes and the symmetry contracts between them.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §0  CONCEPTS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s0">
<h2><span class="sec-num">§0</span> The three-layer model <button class="done-btn" onclick="tick(0,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<p>Connections in Silmulator involve three distinct layers. Confusing them is the root of most bugs:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Data layer</strong> — <code>connected_to</code> pointers on <code>ConnectionPort</code>. These are Python object references. They say "these two ports know about each other".</li>
  <li><strong>Visual layer</strong> — <code>WireItem</code>, a <code>QGraphicsLineItem</code> that paints the yellow line. It reads <code>scenePos()</code> from the two ports to draw itself.</li>
  <li><strong>Signal layer</strong> — <code>pyqtSignal</code> emissions on <code>CanvasScene</code> (<code>wire_added</code>, <code>wire_removed</code>, etc.) that let the rest of the app react without polling.</li>
</ul>
<div class="diagram">
  <div class="d-row">
    <div class="d-block">BlockA<br><span class="d-port cn"></span> OUT1</div>
    <div class="d-wire"></div>
    <div class="d-block">IN1 <span class="d-port cn"></span><br>BlockB</div>
  </div>
  <div class="d-row" style="margin-top:.5rem">
    <span class="d-label">data: port.connected_to → peer</span>
    <span class="d-label" style="margin-left:auto">visual: WireItem.setLine(srcPos, dstPos)</span>
  </div>
</div>
<p>All three must stay in sync. <code>connect_ports()</code> and <code>_remove_wire()</code> are the only two code paths that should touch all three layers — don't update ports directly from outside those methods.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §1  ConnectionPort
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s1">
<h2><span class="sec-num">§1</span> ConnectionPort — the data node <button class="done-btn" onclick="tick(1,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fpill">silmulator/gui/blocks/base_block.py:21–66</div>

<p><code>ConnectionPort</code> inherits from <code>QGraphicsEllipseItem</code>. It is a child item of the block, which means its coordinates are relative to the block's local origin.</p>

<pre>
<span class="fl">29</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, parent: <span class="str">"BaseBlock"</span>, port_id: <span class="cls">str</span>, label: <span class="cls">str</span> = <span class="str">""</span>) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">30</span>    r = self.PORT_RADIUS          <span class="cm"># 6.0 px</span>
<span class="fl">31</span>    <span class="kw">super</span>().<span class="fn">__init__</span>(-r, -r, 2*r, 2*r, parent)   <span class="cm"># centered on local (0, 0)</span>
<span class="fl">32</span>    self.parent_block = parent    <span class="cm"># back-reference to the owning block</span>
<span class="fl">33</span>    self.port_id = port_id        <span class="cm"># unique string within this block e.g. "OUT1"</span>
<span class="fl">34</span>    self.label = label
<span class="fl">35</span>    self.connected_to: Optional[<span class="str">"ConnectionPort"</span>] = <span class="kw">None</span>  <span class="cm"># THE key field</span>
</pre>

<h3>The symmetry invariant</h3>
<p>The cardinal rule: <em>if A.connected_to is B, then B.connected_to must be A</em>. Both sides must always point at each other, or at <code>None</code> together. Break this and you get ghost wires.</p>

<pre>
<span class="fl">54</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">connect_to</span>(self, other: <span class="str">"ConnectionPort"</span>) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">55</span>    self.connected_to = other     <span class="cm"># set both sides at once</span>
<span class="fl">56</span>    other.connected_to = self
<span class="fl">57</span>    self.<span class="fn">_update_color</span>()          <span class="cm"># turn green</span>
<span class="fl">58</span>    other.<span class="fn">_update_color</span>()

<span class="fl">60</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">disconnect</span>(self) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">61</span>    <span class="kw">if</span> self.connected_to:
<span class="fl">62</span>        self.connected_to.connected_to = <span class="kw">None</span>  <span class="cm"># null the peer FIRST</span>
<span class="fl">63</span>        self.connected_to.<span class="fn">_update_color</span>()       <span class="cm"># turn blue again</span>
<span class="fl">64</span>    self.connected_to = <span class="kw">None</span>                    <span class="cm"># then null self</span>
<span class="fl">65</span>    self.<span class="fn">_update_color</span>()
</pre>

<div class="warn">If you call <code>port.connected_to = None</code> directly (skipping <code>disconnect()</code>), the peer still thinks it's connected. The wire disappears from the canvas but the port stays green and <code>_get_connected_blocks()</code> still returns the orphaned peer — a ghost connection.</div>

<h3>Port positioning — fractional coordinates</h3>
<p>Ports are positioned using fractions of the block's width and height:</p>
<pre>
<span class="cm"># base_block.py:191</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">add_port</span>(self, port_id, label, x_frac, y_frac):
    port = <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>(self, port_id, label)
    port.<span class="fn">setPos</span>(self.BLOCK_WIDTH * x_frac, self.BLOCK_HEIGHT * y_frac)
    <span class="cm"># e.g. x_frac=1.0 → right edge, y_frac=0.5 → vertical center</span>
</pre>
<p>Because <code>ConnectionPort</code> is a child of the block, when the block moves, all its ports move with it for free — Qt propagates the transform down to children automatically. The <code>setPos()</code> here is in the block's <em>local</em> coordinate space.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §2  WireItem
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s2">
<h2><span class="sec-num">§2</span> WireItem — the visual layer <button class="done-btn" onclick="tick(2,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fpill">silmulator/gui/canvas.py:46–68</div>

<pre>
<span class="fl">46</span><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">WireItem</span>(<span class="cls">QGraphicsLineItem</span>):
<span class="fl">49</span>    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, src: <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>, dst: <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">50</span>        <span class="kw">super</span>().<span class="fn">__init__</span>()
<span class="fl">51</span>        self.src_port = src
<span class="fl">52</span>        self.dst_port = dst
<span class="fl">53</span>        self.wire_id = str(uuid.<span class="fn">uuid4</span>())[:<span class="num">8</span>]
<span class="fl">54</span>        pen = <span class="cls">QPen</span>(<span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">255</span>, <span class="num">200</span>, <span class="num">50</span>), <span class="num">2</span>, ...)
<span class="fl">57</span>        self.<span class="fn">setZValue</span>(-<span class="num">1</span>)      <span class="cm"># drawn BELOW every block</span>
<span class="fl">58</span>        self.<span class="fn">update_position</span>()  <span class="cm"># draw line immediately</span>
<span class="fl">60</span>    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">update_position</span>(self) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">61</span>        src_pos = self.src_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()  <span class="cm"># scene coords, not local</span>
<span class="fl">62</span>        dst_pos = self.dst_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()
<span class="fl">63</span>        self.<span class="fn">setLine</span>(<span class="cls">QLineF</span>(src_pos, dst_pos))
</pre>

<h3>Why ZValue = −1?</h3>
<p>Qt paints items in order of their ZValue (lower = painted first = visually behind). Blocks have ZValue 0 (the default). Setting wires to −1 ensures that when two blocks overlap, the wire between them doesn't appear drawn over the block's body and title bar.</p>

<h3>Why <code>scenePos()</code> and not <code>pos()</code>?</h3>
<p><code>pos()</code> returns coordinates in the item's parent's local space. For a <code>ConnectionPort</code> that is a child of a block, <code>pos()</code> gives you something like <code>(220, 80)</code> — relative to the block. <code>scenePos()</code> walks up the parent chain and gives you the absolute scene position, which is what you need to draw a straight line across the canvas between two ports on two different blocks.</p>

<h3>update_position() is called during mouse drag</h3>
<p>While the user is dragging a block, Qt moves the block and all its child ports — but it doesn't automatically re-draw any <code>WireItem</code>s. That's why <code>mouseMoveEvent</code> calls <code>wire.update_position()</code> for every wire on every mouse move during a wire-drag gesture:</p>
<pre>
<span class="cm"># canvas.py:238–245</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> self._connecting <span class="kw">and</span> self._temp_wire:
    self._temp_wire.<span class="fn">setLine</span>(...)
    <span class="kw">for</span> wire <span class="kw">in</span> self._wires.values():
        wire.<span class="fn">update_position</span>()  <span class="cm"># re-query scenePos() to keep wires taut</span>
    event.<span class="fn">accept</span>()
    <span class="kw">return</span>
</pre>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §3  Mouse state machine
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s3">
<h2><span class="sec-num">§3</span> The mouse state machine <button class="done-btn" onclick="tick(3,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fpill">silmulator/gui/canvas.py:225–287</div>

<p>Drawing a wire is a three-event gesture. The scene holds two pieces of mutable state that drive it:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>self._connecting: Optional[ConnectionPort]</code> — the source port being dragged from, or <code>None</code>.</li>
  <li><code>self._temp_wire: Optional[QGraphicsLineItem]</code> — the dashed rubber-band line that follows the cursor, or <code>None</code>.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sm">
  <span class="sm-ev">mousePressEvent</span>
  <span class="sm-desc">Call <code>_port_at(scenePos)</code>. If a free port is found: save it as <code>_connecting</code>, create a dashed <code>_temp_wire</code> at ZValue 100, call <code>event.accept()</code> to block rubber-band selection.</span>
  <span class="sm-ev">mouseMoveEvent</span>
  <span class="sm-desc">If <code>_connecting</code>: update <code>_temp_wire</code> endpoint to follow cursor, re-run <code>update_position()</code> on all committed wires, <code>event.accept()</code>.</span>
  <span class="sm-ev">mouseReleaseEvent</span>
  <span class="sm-desc">Call <code>_port_at(scenePos)</code> for the target. If a valid different port is found: call <code>connect_ports(src, target)</code> to create the real wire. Always call <code>_cancel_wire()</code> to remove the temp wire and reset state.</span>
</div>

<h3>_port_at() — finding the port under the cursor</h3>
<pre>
<span class="fl">261</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_port_at</span>(self, pos) -&gt; Optional[<span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>]:
<span class="fl">262</span>    <span class="kw">for</span> item <span class="kw">in</span> self.<span class="fn">items</span>(pos):    <span class="cm"># returns items at pos, front to back</span>
<span class="fl">263</span>        <span class="kw">if</span> item <span class="kw">is</span> self._temp_wire: <span class="cm"># skip the rubber-band line itself</span>
<span class="fl">264</span>            <span class="kw">continue</span>
<span class="fl">265</span>        <span class="kw">if</span> isinstance(item, <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>):
<span class="fl">266</span>            <span class="kw">return</span> item
<span class="fl">267</span>    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>
</pre>
<p><code>self.items(pos)</code> returns every item whose bounding rect contains <code>pos</code>, ordered from topmost Z to bottommost. The temp wire sits at ZValue 100 — above everything — so without the <code>is self._temp_wire</code> skip, it would always be the first item returned and the target port would never be found on release.</p>

<h3>Why event.accept() is critical</h3>
<pre>
<span class="cm"># canvas.py:228–234</span>
port = self.<span class="fn">_port_at</span>(event.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>())
<span class="kw">if</span> port <span class="kw">is not</span> <span class="kw">None</span> <span class="kw">and</span> port.connected_to <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">None</span>:
    self.<span class="fn">_start_wire</span>(port, event.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>())
    event.<span class="fn">accept</span>()   <span class="cm"># &lt;— THIS LINE IS LOAD-BEARING</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span>
</pre>
<div class="warn">If <code>event.accept()</code> is removed, the event propagates up to <code>CanvasView</code>, which is in <code>RubberBandDrag</code> mode. The view starts a rubber-band selection rectangle that fights the dashed wire on every mouse move, churns the scene selection, and prevents the wire from being drawn. This was the original root-cause bug fixed in commit <code>34122c2</code>.</div>

<h3>The temp wire is non-interactive</h3>
<pre>
<span class="fl">278</span>self._temp_wire.<span class="fn">setAcceptedMouseButtons</span>(<span class="cls">Qt</span>.MouseButton.NoButton)
<span class="fl">279</span>self._temp_wire.<span class="fn">setFlag</span>(
<span class="fl">280</span>    <span class="cls">QGraphicsItem</span>.GraphicsItemFlag.ItemIsSelectable, <span class="kw">False</span>
<span class="fl">281</span>)
</pre>
<p>These two lines ensure the temp wire is invisible to mouse hit-testing. Without them, the temp wire (ZValue 100) would eat every subsequent <code>mouseMoveEvent</code> hit and <code>_port_at()</code> could never find the destination port underneath.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §4  connect_ports & _remove_wire
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s4">
<h2><span class="sec-num">§4</span> connect_ports() and _remove_wire() <button class="done-btn" onclick="tick(4,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fpill">silmulator/gui/canvas.py:195–219</div>

<h3>connect_ports — creating a permanent wire</h3>
<pre>
<span class="fl">195</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">connect_ports</span>(self, src, dst) -&gt; Optional[<span class="cls">WireItem</span>]:
<span class="fl">196</span>    <span class="kw">if</span> src.parent_block <span class="kw">is</span> dst.parent_block:
<span class="fl">197</span>        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>   <span class="cm"># self-loop guard</span>
<span class="fl">198</span>    <span class="kw">if</span> src.connected_to <span class="kw">or</span> dst.connected_to:
<span class="fl">199</span>        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>   <span class="cm"># already-connected guard</span>
<span class="fl">201</span>    wire = <span class="cls">WireItem</span>(src, dst)    <span class="cm"># creates the visual</span>
<span class="fl">202</span>    self.<span class="fn">addItem</span>(wire)           <span class="cm"># registers with the scene</span>
<span class="fl">203</span>    self._wires[wire.wire_id] = wire   <span class="cm"># registers in our dict</span>
<span class="fl">204</span>    src.<span class="fn">connect_to</span>(dst)         <span class="cm"># updates the data layer (both ports)</span>
<span class="fl">205</span>    self.wire_added.<span class="fn">emit</span>(...)   <span class="cm"># signal layer</span>
<span class="fl">208</span>    <span class="kw">return</span> wire
</pre>

<p>Notice the order: the <code>WireItem</code> is created <em>before</em> calling <code>connect_to()</code>. This is safe because <code>WireItem.__init__</code> only reads port positions — it doesn't check <code>connected_to</code>. The data layer is updated <em>after</em> the visual exists and is added to the scene.</p>

<h3>_remove_wire — teardown order matters</h3>
<pre>
<span class="fl">214</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_remove_wire</span>(self, wire: <span class="cls">WireItem</span>) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">215</span>    wire.src_port.<span class="fn">disconnect</span>()      <span class="cm"># nulls both src and dst (data layer)</span>
<span class="fl">216</span>    wire.dst_port.<span class="fn">disconnect</span>()      <span class="cm"># safe to call twice — already None</span>
<span class="fl">217</span>    self._wires.<span class="fn">pop</span>(wire.wire_id, <span class="kw">None</span>)
<span class="fl">218</span>    self.<span class="fn">removeItem</span>(wire)           <span class="cm"># removes from scene (visual layer)</span>
<span class="fl">219</span>    self.wire_removed.<span class="fn">emit</span>(wire.wire_id)
</pre>

<p>Why call <code>disconnect()</code> on <em>both</em> ports? Because <code>disconnect()</code> nulls the <em>peer</em> first, then self. After <code>src.disconnect()</code> runs: src.connected_to=None AND dst.connected_to=None. The second call <code>dst.disconnect()</code> is idempotent — it sees <code>connected_to is None</code> and does nothing. This double-call pattern is defensive: it costs nothing and prevents the ghost-connection bug even if the caller gets the order slightly wrong.</p>

<div class="key">Pattern to remember: in any teardown that involves bidirectional references, null the cross-references first, then remove from registries, then remove from the parent container. Data → dict → scene.</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §5  Serialisation
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s5">
<h2><span class="sec-num">§5</span> Save &amp; load — the port_map bridge <button class="done-btn" onclick="tick(5,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fpill">silmulator/gui/canvas.py:323–357</div>

<p>A wire references two <code>ConnectionPort</code> objects — live Python objects that don't survive to disk. Serialisation solves this with two steps: encoding the reference as a (block_id, port_id) pair, then rebuilding the live objects via a lookup table.</p>

<h3>to_dict — encode wires as ID pairs</h3>
<pre>
<span class="cm"># Each wire is stored as four strings, not Python references:</span>
{
    <span class="str">"src_block"</span>: wire.src_port.parent_block.block_id,
    <span class="str">"src_port"</span>:  wire.src_port.port_id,
    <span class="str">"dst_block"</span>: wire.dst_port.parent_block.block_id,
    <span class="str">"dst_port"</span>:  wire.dst_port.port_id,
}
</pre>

<h3>from_dict — rebuild via port_map</h3>
<pre>
<span class="fl">344</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">from_dict</span>(self, data) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">345</span>    self.<span class="fn">clear</span>()
<span class="fl">346</span>    port_map: Dict[Tuple[<span class="cls">str</span>, <span class="cls">str</span>], <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>] = {}
<span class="fl">347</span>    <span class="kw">for</span> bdata <span class="kw">in</span> data[<span class="str">"blocks"</span>]:
<span class="fl">348</span>        block = self.<span class="fn">add_block</span>(bdata[<span class="str">"type"</span>], ...)
<span class="fl">350</span>        <span class="kw">for</span> port <span class="kw">in</span> block.ports:
<span class="fl">351</span>            port_map[(bdata[<span class="str">"id"</span>], port.port_id)] = port  <span class="cm"># build the bridge</span>
<span class="fl">352</span>    <span class="kw">for</span> wdata <span class="kw">in</span> data[<span class="str">"wires"</span>]:
<span class="fl">353</span>        src = port_map.<span class="fn">get</span>((wdata[<span class="str">"src_block"</span>], wdata[<span class="str">"src_port"</span>]))
<span class="fl">354</span>        dst = port_map.<span class="fn">get</span>((wdata[<span class="str">"dst_block"</span>], wdata[<span class="str">"dst_port"</span>]))
<span class="fl">355</span>        <span class="kw">if</span> src <span class="kw">and</span> dst:
<span class="fl">356</span>            self.<span class="fn">connect_ports</span>(src, dst)  <span class="cm"># reconnects via the normal path</span>
</pre>

<p><code>port_map</code> is keyed by a tuple: <code>(block_id_string, port_id_string)</code>. The block IDs come from <code>to_dict</code>'s <code>bdata["id"]</code> field, not the freshly-created block's ID — that matters because <code>add_block</code> generates a new <code>uuid</code>. So <code>from_dict</code> must use the <em>saved</em> block ID as the key, not the live one.</p>

<div class="note">The <code>if src and dst</code> guard is why wires silently disappear on reload when a block type is missing from <code>BLOCK_REGISTRY</code>: <code>add_block()</code> returns <code>None</code>, the block is never added to <code>port_map</code>, and the wire lookup fails quietly. The original "Function Gen" vs "Function Generator" mismatch bug manifested exactly this way.</div>

<h3>clear() — why it must reset Python dicts</h3>
<pre>
<span class="fl">178</span><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">clear</span>(self) -&gt; <span class="cls">None</span>:
<span class="fl">185</span>    <span class="kw">super</span>().<span class="fn">clear</span>()    <span class="cm"># deletes C++ items — Python dicts still hold references</span>
<span class="fl">186</span>    self._blocks = {}  <span class="cm"># must reset or next save hits deleted C++ objects</span>
<span class="fl">187</span>    self._wires = {}
<span class="fl">188</span>    self._connecting = <span class="kw">None</span>
<span class="fl">189</span>    self._temp_wire = <span class="kw">None</span>
</pre>
<p><code>QGraphicsScene.clear()</code> destroys the underlying C++ objects for every item. Python objects wrapping them become invalid — any attribute access raises <code>RuntimeError: wrapped C/C++ object has been deleted</code>. Resetting the dicts drops the Python references so the wrappers are garbage-collected cleanly.</p>
</section>

