<section id="s6">
<h2><span class="sec-num">§6</span> Practice <button class="done-btn" onclick="tick(6,this)">Mark done</button></h2>

<div class="quiz">
<h3>Quick-fire quiz</h3>

<!-- Q1 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="1">
<div class="qt">Q1. A port's <code>connected_to</code> is set but its peer's <code>connected_to</code> is <code>None</code>. What do we call this state?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="An orphaned block is one that has no ports connected at all. This is about a broken bidirectional pointer — both sides exist, but the symmetry is violated.">Orphaned block</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. When A.connected_to=B but B.connected_to=None, B thinks it's free while A thinks it's connected. Routing code that walks connected_to will behave differently depending on which port it starts from — this is a ghost connection.">Ghost connection — the symmetry invariant is broken</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="A dangling pointer is the right idea (a pointer to a non-live object), but here both Python objects are alive. The problem is the asymmetry: one side knows about the connection, the other doesn't.">Dangling pointer — the port object was deleted</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="A stale wire is a WireItem that still exists in the scene after its ports were disconnected. This is a different (visual) invariant violation. The question is about the data layer.">Stale wire — the WireItem outlived its ports</li>
</ul>
<div class="fb" id="fb1"></div>
</div>

<!-- Q2 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="2">
<div class="qt">Q2. You call <code>_port_at(scenePos)</code> during <code>mouseReleaseEvent</code> and it always returns <code>None</code>, even when the cursor is clearly over a destination port. What is the most likely cause?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. _temp_wire sits at ZValue 100, above everything. items(pos) returns it first. Without the 'if item is self._temp_wire: continue' skip, _port_at() would stop at the temp wire and never reach the ConnectionPort underneath — returning None.">The temp wire (ZValue 100) is above the port and eating the hit test — the <code>_temp_wire</code> skip is missing or broken.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="ConnectionPort inherits from QGraphicsEllipseItem, which accepts hover events by default. Missing setAcceptHoverEvents wouldn't stop items() from returning the port — hover events and hit-testing are different mechanisms.">The destination port's <code>setAcceptHoverEvents(True)</code> was not called, so it's excluded from hit-testing.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="Port position is relative to the block. scenePos() translates it correctly. A fractional x/y bug would misplace the port visually, but the visual mismatch would be obvious; it wouldn't cause items() to miss the port at the cursor location.">The port's <code>x_frac</code> / <code>y_frac</code> placement is wrong, so <code>scenePos()</code> returns a position far from the cursor.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="QGraphicsScene.items(pos) returns all items whose bounding rect contains pos — it doesn't require ItemIsSelectable. The selectable flag only affects rubber-band selection and scene.selectedItems().">The destination port has <code>ItemIsSelectable</code> set to <code>False</code>, so <code>items(pos)</code> skips it.</li>
</ul>
<div class="fb" id="fb2"></div>
</div>

<!-- Q3 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="3">
<div class="qt">Q3. You add a new block type but after save → reload, all wires connected to it vanish silently. The JSON file contains the wires. What went wrong?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="update_position() is only called during mouse moves and when a block is dragged. It has nothing to do with the save/reload path, and a missing call wouldn't cause wires to disappear.">The new block's <code>update_position()</code> was not called during reload, so wires have zero length and are invisible.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="clear() resets _blocks and _wires correctly regardless of block type. The bug is not in the reset step — it's in add_block() during from_dict().">The scene's <code>clear()</code> override doesn't reset <code>_blocks</code>, so port_map gets stale entries from the previous session.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. from_dict() calls add_block(bdata['type']). add_block() does BLOCK_REGISTRY.get(device_type). If the string doesn't match a key (e.g. 'Function Gen' vs 'Function Generator'), it returns None and the block is never created. port_map has no entry for it, so the wire lookup (port_map.get(...)) returns None, and the if src and dst: guard skips the connect_ports() call — wires silently dropped.">The <code>device_type</code> string doesn't match any key in <code>BLOCK_REGISTRY</code>, so <code>add_block()</code> returns <code>None</code> and the block is never added to <code>port_map</code>.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="WireItem is a QGraphicsLineItem, not a QGraphicsItem subclass with a custom shape(). Even if shape() were wrong, the wire would be created and drawn — just with the wrong hit area.">The new block's <code>shape()</code> method returns an incorrect <code>QPainterPath</code>, preventing wires from attaching to it.</li>
</ul>
