</div>

<!-- Q4 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="4">
<div class="qt">Q4. <code>WireItem.update_position()</code> calls <code>scenePos()</code> on both ports. Why not <code>pos()</code>?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both pos() and scenePos() work on live items. The issue is coordinate space, not object lifetime.">Because <code>pos()</code> raises an exception on child items whose parent has been moved since the item was added.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="scenePos() does not involve pixel rounding — it returns QPointF in floating-point scene units. The anti-aliasing decision is made in the QPainter, not in coordinate retrieval.">Because <code>pos()</code> returns integer pixels, while <code>scenePos()</code> returns floating-point values that support anti-aliased rendering.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. pos() returns the item's position in its parent's local coordinate space. For a ConnectionPort (child of a BaseBlock), pos() gives you something like (220, 80) — relative to the block's top-left corner. To draw a line from one block's port to another block's port, you need absolute scene coordinates, which scenePos() provides by walking up the parent transform chain.">Because <code>pos()</code> returns local coordinates relative to the item's parent (the block), but the wire needs absolute scene coordinates to draw a line between two different blocks.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="scenePos() is available on any QGraphicsItem, including QGraphicsEllipseItem (which ConnectionPort inherits from). It's not a privilege of specific subclasses.">Because <code>scenePos()</code> is only available on <code>QGraphicsObject</code> subclasses, and <code>ConnectionPort</code> inherits from <code>QGraphicsEllipseItem</code> which is a <code>QGraphicsObject</code>.</li>
</ul>
