<div class="qblock" data-q="5">
<div class="qt">Q5. In <code>_remove_wire()</code>, why is <code>wire.dst_port.disconnect()</code> called after <code>wire.src_port.disconnect()</code>, given that <code>src.disconnect()</code> already nulls the peer?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="disconnect() always calls _update_color() — it works correctly whether connected_to is set or None. The color isn't the reason for the second call.">To ensure <code>dst_port._update_color()</code> runs, since <code>src_port.disconnect()</code> only updates the source port's color.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. src.disconnect() nulls both src.connected_to AND dst.connected_to. Calling dst.disconnect() afterwards is idempotent — it sees connected_to=None and does nothing. The double-call is defensive: if the code ever changes so that src.disconnect() doesn't null the peer, the second call prevents a ghost connection. Zero runtime cost, strong correctness guarantee.">It's a defensive invariant: the second call is idempotent (does nothing when <code>connected_to</code> is already <code>None</code>) but guards against future changes to <code>disconnect()</code> that might not null both sides.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both ports are still live Python objects at this point. removeItem() hasn't been called yet. Neither port is in any state that would require 'cleanup before deletion'.">Because <code>removeItem()</code> deletes the C++ item, and the second <code>disconnect()</code> must run before that to prevent a RuntimeError on the C++ object.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="There is no ordered return convention in Python for cleanup calls here. Both ports are fully accessible throughout _remove_wire(). The src/dst order in the function signature doesn't impose any requirement on teardown order.">Because the function signature guarantees src was set before dst, so teardown must happen in the same order to respect Python's reference-counting semantics.</li>
</ul>
