<!-- ── scenarios ── -->
<h3 style="margin-top:2rem;color:#e6edf3">Scenario questions</h3>

<div class="scenario">
  <div class="sc-q">A user reports that wires "snap back" and disappear the moment they release the mouse on a destination port. The dashed line was visible during the drag. What do you check first?</div>
  <button class="sc-reveal" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
  <div class="sc-ans">Check <code>connect_ports()</code>'s guards (canvas.py:196–199). The most likely cause is that the destination port is already connected (<code>dst.connected_to is not None</code>), triggering the early <code>return None</code>. The wire drag completes successfully and <code>_cancel_wire()</code> removes the temp wire — but <code>connect_ports()</code> silently returns without creating a WireItem. To confirm: right-click the destination port's block and check its command log. If the port is wired, its commands will appear. Also check whether a previous session's wire is still recorded (stale <code>_wires</code> dict after File→New was the original bug).</div>
</div>

<div class="scenario">
  <div class="sc-q">After adding a new custom block class, you notice that moving the block leaves its wire endpoints frozen in the original position — the wire doesn't follow the block. The wire connects this block's port to a Power Supply port. What is missing?</div>
  <button class="sc-reveal" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
  <div class="sc-ans"><code>update_position()</code> is only called inside the <code>if self._connecting and self._temp_wire</code> branch of <code>mouseMoveEvent</code>. This means wires only update during active wire-drawing gestures, not during block drags. To fix this properly, you need to call <code>wire.update_position()</code> whenever a block moves — the right place is to override <code>itemChange()</code> on <code>BaseBlock</code> with <code>GraphicsItemChange.ItemPositionHasChanged</code>: look up all wires touching this block's ports and call <code>update_position()</code>. Alternatively, connect to a signal on the block.</div>
</div>

<div class="scenario">
  <div class="sc-q">You want to write a unit test that checks: after <code>scene.clear()</code>, calling <code>scene.to_dict()</code> returns <code>{"blocks": [], "wires": []}</code>. The test passes in isolation but crashes with <code>RuntimeError: wrapped C/C++ object has been deleted</code> in the full suite. Why?</div>
  <button class="sc-reveal" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
  <div class="sc-ans">The crash happens when another test calls <code>scene.to_dict()</code> or accesses <code>scene.blocks</code> after <code>QGraphicsScene.clear()</code> has been called on the same scene instance — but before the Python dicts (<code>_blocks</code>, <code>_wires</code>) were reset. The overridden <code>clear()</code> fixes this by resetting the dicts immediately after <code>super().clear()</code>. If the test suite reuses the same scene object between tests (a common pattern for speed), it must call the overridden <code>clear()</code>, not <code>QGraphicsScene.clear()</code> directly. The fix: ensure each test that needs a clean scene calls <code>scene.clear()</code> (our override) and verify that <code>len(scene.blocks) == 0</code> before proceeding.</div>
</div>
</section>

